  "id": "Compound::DB00252 - Gene::6329",
  "verbalization": "Phenytoin's relationship with Scn4a is mediated through its inclusion in a group of cytochrome P450 inducers, specifically Carbamazepine, which binds to Scn4a. This pathway is consistent across multiple visible paths, as Phenytoin is classified as an inducer of Cytochrome P450 1a2, 2c9, and 2b6, all of which are associated with Carbamazepine. The shared mechanism of action involves the induction of metabolic pathways that lead to the increased production of Carbamazepine, which in turn binds to Scn4a. The lowest common ancestor of Phenytoin and Carbamazepine is classified as an Anticonvulsant Agent and an Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound, reinforcing the broader pharmacological context of this interaction. The evidence suggests a direct, albeit indirect, relationship through the metabolic pathway involving Cytochrome P450 enzymes, with the binding of Carbamazepine to Scn4a as a critical step in the mechanism.",
